20. SEGMENT ISOLATION PRINCIPLES
Every product belongs to exactly one segment ID
Every buyer workspace belongs to one segment
One buyer account can have multiple segment business profiles
Search results always filtered by segment_id
Recommendations always segment-specific
Admin sees all segments but filtered views
Supplier can belong to multiple segments but segment access controlled separately
Segment switching is explicit, not context-switching
Catalog attributes defined per segment schema
Pricing rules per segment
Return rules per segment
Compliance rules per segment
21. SECURITY BASELINE
Principles
Least privilege access
Segment isolation enforced at DB query layer
Input validation on every endpoint
Rate limiting per user/IP
File upload: type/size validation, private buckets, signed URLs
Password hashing: Argon2id
Session: JWT + refresh rotation
Device/session tracking
Strict RBAC enforcement
Audit trail for all admin actions
Threat Prevention
Threat	Prevention
SQL Injection	Prisma parameterized queries
XSS	Output encoding, CSP
CSRF	SameSite cookies + CSRF tokens
API Abuse	Rate limiting
Account Hijacking	OTP + device tracking
Segment Data Leakage	Row-level security, segment_id on every query
Unauthorized Access	RBAC middleware
Scraping	Rate limits + bot detection
Malicious Uploads	File type validation, size limits
Payment Abuse	Transaction limits + audit
22. SCALABILITY PRINCIPLES
Stateless API servers (no session stored on server)
Docker containerized deployments
Horizontal scaling possible (cloud)
CDN for static assets
Redis for caching + sessions
Queue-based async processing
Database query optimization (index strategy)
Read replica ready (future, not required now)
Media optimization (compression, thumbnails, CDN)
Lazy loading for images and data
Optimistic locking for inventory
Database connection pooling
23. DEPLOYMENT PRINCIPLES
Docker for local/staging/production parity
GitHub Actions for CI/CD
Public cloud (AWS/Hetzner)
Environment variables for configuration
Environment separation: dev/staging/prod
Secrets management (AWS Secrets Manager / .env)
Database migrations automated (Prisma)
Rollback plan for every deployment
Feature flags for risky releases
Staging testing mandatory before prod
24. OPERATIONAL PHILOSOPHY
Minimum manual work
Maximum data capture
AI-ready data from Day 1
Workflow automation where possible
But humans in loop for critical decisions initially
No blind AI automation in MVP
Trust before scale
Profit before GMV
Control before marketplace
Data before AI
Operations first, technology second
25. CHANGE CONTROL POLICY — MANDATORY
After PRDv2 freeze, any new feature or architecture change must pass:

Step 1: Impact Analysis
Document:

Operational impact?
UX impact?
Database impact?
Infra impact?
Scalability impact?
Workflow impact?
Security impact?
Cost impact?
Step 2: Review Board
At least 2 stakeholders must approve
Approval documented
Step 3: Integration Check
Does it break existing workflows?
Does it impact database schema?
Does it require data migration?
Does it conflict with architecture constraints?
Step 4: UX Review
Does it add cognitive load?
Does it confuse existing users?
Does it fit Bharat UX philosophy?
Step 5: Operational Review
Can operations team handle this?
Does it require new training?
Does it create manual work?
Step 6: Security Review
New threat surface?
Data leakage risk?
Permission escalation risk?
Rule
NO new feature approved without written impact analysis and stakeholder approval. No exceptions.

